  {
    "model_name": "Boeing 247",
    "date": "7 Oct 1935",
    "type": "Boeing 247D",
    "operator": "United Airlines",
    "location": "near Cheyenne, WY",
    "category": "",
    "fatalities": 12,
    "narrative": "United Trip 4, Boeing 247D, struck a hill during a night-time approach to Cheyenne, Wyoming, USA. All 12 on board were killed. \n \nThis flight, scheduled as Trip 4 was dispatched from Salt Lake City for Cheyenne at 12:07 a.m. The weather was practically clear. Normal radio contacts were made throughout the flight, the last one being a contact at 02:16 by the co-pilot with Cheyenne, giving the airplane's position as \"Silver Crown\", which is approximately 3 miles west of the point where the accident occurred. This was the last radio contact with the flight.  \nInvestigation disclosed that the airplane, while on the direct radio course to the Cheyenne Airport and while flying in a normal, slightly descending path, had first collided with the ground just a few feet below the top pf a small knoll tearing out both engines and engine nacelles. From here it had rebounded and continued through the air for a distance of 1120 feet, where it came to rest.  \n \nIt is the opinion of the Accident Board that the probable cause of this accident was an error on the part of the pilot judging his altitude or his distance from the airport or both. \n\n\nICAO Occurrence Category:\n \n\n\nCFIT\n: Controlled Flight Into or Toward Terrain\r\nAccident investigation:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\u00a0\n\u00a0\n\n\nInvestigating\u00a0agency:\u00a0\nCAB \n\n\nReport\u00a0number:\u00a0\n \n\n\nStatus: \nInvestigation completed\n\n\nDuration: \n\n\nDownload report: \n \n \nFinal report",
    "asn_url": "https://aviation-safety.net/wikibase/34145"
  },
  {
    "model_name": "Boeing 247",
    "date": "30 Oct 1935",
    "type": "Boeing 247D",
    "operator": "United Airlines",
    "location": "5 mi S of Cheyenne, WY",
    "category": "",
    "fatalities": 4,
    "narrative": "Purpose of the flight was checking performance of the airplane, instruments and engines after a routine change of engines and instruments. There was two pilots and two engineers on board.  \nThe weather at the time of the takeoff: \nceiling - 1200 feet, visibility - 15 miles, light snow, icing. \nCrashed while performing right turn. Nose drop and dived at the angle of 70 degrees. Destroyed by the post crash fire.",
    "asn_url": "https://aviation-safety.net/wikibase/24776"
  },
