  {
    "model_name": "Boeing 707-300",
    "date": "16 Aug 1980",
    "type": "Boeing 707-340C",
    "operator": "JAT Yugoslav Airlines",
    "location": "Stockholm-Arlanda Airport (ARN/ESSA)",
    "category": "",
    "fatalities": 0,
    "narrative": "JAT Yugoslav Airlines flight JU362, a Boeing 707C, YU-AGG, suffered a runway excursion during landing at Stockholm Arlanda airport. The aircraft suffered minor damage. None of the 93 passengers and crew were injured. \n \nA Yugoslav passenger aircraft, YU-AGG, a Boeing 707 C owned by JAT, ran off the runway during landing at Arlanda Airport on 16 August 1980 after passing through a thunderstorm at the end of the runway. \n \nThe aircraft was in regular service, route designation JU 362, Belgrade - Stockholm with an intermediate landing in Copenhagen. On board were seven crew members and 86 passengers. The flight from Belgrade to the Arlanda terminal area went without a problem. Stockholm control cleared the aircraft for approach to runway 26 \"via Trosa Three Arrival\". The following weather was reported for runway 26 at that time: Wind direction variable 3 knots, horizontal visibility 10 km, 2/8 CB 3,000 ft, temperature 220, dew point 14\u00b0 and QNH 1024. \n \nThe weather in the Stockholm area in general was characterized by a weak high pressure ridge that extended from the Kola Peninsula SW down over central and southern Sweden. Visibility was good and the cloud cover consisted of 1-3/8 CU. A weakly developed cold front was moving slowly from the east over Uppland. A satellite image from 1309 showed that the front consisted of scattered convective clouds with a fairly intense thundercloud in the area of \u200b\u200bArlanda. The air traffic controller in the Arlanda tower perceived that there were scattered black clouds around the airport and that it was black to the east. According to available information, a cumulonimbus cloud (CB) with a heavy rain shower passed from the east over runway 26 at the same time as JAT landed. \n \nAfter YU-AGG had followed Trosa Three Arrival for a short distance, Stockholm control initiated a radar route of the aircraft to runway 26. The reason for this was that the radar had detected a CB cloud approximately 11-12 nautical miles from the runway. The aircraft was directed away from the cloud and turned up on final to runway 26 approximately ten nautical miles from the touchdown point. At approximately six nautical miles, YU-AGG was handed over to the Arlanda tower. \n \nThe crew of YU-AGG had no idea that there were any special weather problems during the radar route and during the first part of the flight. At the same time as YU-AGG was making its approach to runway 26, a SAS DC-9, SK 526, was making an approach for landing on runway 19. When the SAS plane contacted the Arlanda tower at 1311, the air traffic controller looked at the wind gauge that was set for runway 26. This, which for two hours had shown varying winds up to three knots (i.e. almost no wind) now showed 25 knots from 60\u00b0, i.e. just over 20 knots of tailwind for the landing on runway 26. The air traffic controller switched the wind gauge to the gauge for runway 19 and found that there was still no wind there. \n \nShortly after the conversation, AD1 was contacted by YU-AGG who announced that he was on an \"ILS approach\". AD1 then requested JU 362 to continue its approach (continue approach), which JU 362 acknowledged with: \"Continue approach 362\". Immediately afterwards, AD1 contacted the radar controller (TE) for consultation. \n \nWhile AD1 was in contact with SK 526, JU 362 approached runway 26 and during the short conversation between AD1 and TE, JU 362 disappeared into a cloud near the end of the runway. AD1 therefore turned on the approach and runway lights to 100% intensity and asked YU-AGG if they could see the runway. After an affirmative answer, AD1 gave landing clearance and wind direction and wind speed, which had now dropped to 11 knots. \n \nOn board the aircraft, the approach was perceived as routine until the aircraft was at an altitude of approximately 1000 ft QNH (approximately 850 ft Above the runway), just over a minute before touchdown, when it flew into an area with light rain and light turbulence. Approximately at the same time as the aircraft was given landing clearance, the aircraft experienced a sudden, severe turbulence disturbance at the same time as the rain increased sharply. \n \nThe turbulence disturbance was short-lived and the captain chose to continue the approach. Just before touchdown, when visibility was poor, the cloud cover was low and heavy rain was falling (it was dark as if it was night), the crew experienced an increase in speed and got the impression that the tailwind was stronger than expected. The captain had failed to control the aircraft in the roll-over direction and therefore chose to continue the landing. After touchdown, the captain attempted to brake using wheel braking, spoilers and reversing. However, the braking effect was poor and the crew was unable to stop the aircraft on the available runway length. By using emergency braking, the aircraft stopped on the grassy extension of the runway approximately 190 m beyond the runway threshold and approximately 40 m before the slope down to the service road that passes the western end of the runway (See Fig 1). The captain turned the aircraft to the left to avoid a collision with the localizer antenna. \n \nInvestigation Results: \n1. The captain had a valid certificate for the flight. \n \n2. Brake tests showed that the aircraft's brake system was working. \n \n3. The aircraft flew into a CB cloud just before landing and experienced problems with visibility, steering and braking. The ground on the main deck indicates that the aircraft had hydroplaned. \n \n4. It was known that there were CB clouds in the vicinity of Arlanda. The radar controller guided the aircraft away from a CB echo on the final to runway 26. Special information about the CB on the approach. The approach sector was not provided. \n \nProbable Cause: \nJust before landing on runway 26, the aircraft flew into a CB cloud. Strong tailwinds, reduced visibility during approach and rain with hydroplaning made it impossible for the crew to stop on the available runway length. Lack of information about dangerous tailwinds may have been a contributing factor to the approach not being aborted. \n \nSafety Recommendations; \n1. The Civil Aviation Administration and the Swedish Meteorological and Hydrological Institute (SMHI) should investigate whether the information provided to air traffic controllers and meteorologists regarding risks associated with approaches in and under CB clouds can be improved. \n \n2. The incident underlines the importance of improved cooperation between meteorologists, air traffic controllers and pilots regarding weather situations that may pose flight safety risks. The Commission supports the Civil Aviation Administration's work to resolve the problems and believes that this work should be prioritized. (Compare recommendations submitted in accident reports regarding SE-FOZ, SE-IDD, SE-DBI and OY-DLV). \n \n3. The acquisition of modern weather radar combined with appropriate routines for weather surveillance and reporting should be prioritized. \n \n4. SMHI should, in cooperation with the Civil Aviation Authority, review the provisions for the use of supplementary information to MET REPORT, especially when intersecting trajectories are used. The observer's knowledge of the provisions must be kept up to date in an appropriate manner.\n\n\nAccident investigation:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\u00a0\n\u00a0\n\n\nInvestigating\u00a0agency:\u00a0\nSHK \n\n\nReport\u00a0number:\u00a0\n11/80 \n\n\nStatus: \nInvestigation completed\n\n\nDuration: \n11 months\n\n\nDownload report: \n \n \nFinal report",
    "asn_url": "https://aviation-safety.net/wikibase/423013"
  },
